# MCP Tools: Jina AI

ALWAYS include `arguments` in CallMcpTool calls. NEVER send empty `{}`.

### capture_screenshot_url
Capture high-quality screenshots of web pages in base64 encoded JPEG format. Use this tool when you need to visually inspect a website, take a snapshot for analysis, or show users what a webpage looks like.

| Field | Type | Required | Default | Description |
|-------|------|----------|---------|-------------|
| `url` | string | yes |  | The complete HTTP/HTTPS URL of the webpage to capture (e.g., 'https://example.com') |
| `firstScreenOnly` | boolean | no | `false` | Set to true for a single screen capture (faster), false for full page capture including content below the fold |
| `return_url` | boolean | no | `false` | Set to true to return screenshot URLs instead of downloading images as base64 |

Example:
CallMcpTool({ server: "Jina AI", toolName: "capture_screenshot_url", arguments: {"url": "https://example.com"} })

### classify_text
Classify texts into user-defined labels using Jina embeddings. Use this when you need to categorize, tag, or sort text content into predefined categories. Perfect for sentiment analysis, topic classification, content moderation, or any text categorization task.

| Field | Type | Required | Default | Description |
|-------|------|----------|---------|-------------|
| `texts` | array<string> | yes |  | Array of text strings to classify (e.g., ['I love this product', 'terrible experience']) |
| `labels` | array<string> | yes |  | Array of classification labels (e.g., ['positive', 'negative', 'neutral']) |
| `model` | string | no | `"jina-embeddings-v5-text-small"` | Model to use for classification (default: jina-embeddings-v5-text-small) |

Example:
CallMcpTool({ server: "Jina AI", toolName: "classify_text", arguments: {"texts": ["value"], "labels": ["value"]} })

### deduplicate_images
Get top-k semantically unique images (URLs or base64-encoded) using Jina CLIP v2 embeddings and submodular optimization. Use this when you have many visually similar images and want the most diverse subset.

| Field | Type | Required | Default | Description |
|-------|------|----------|---------|-------------|
| `images` | array<string> | yes |  | Array of image inputs to deduplicate. Each item can be either an HTTP(S) URL or a raw base64-encoded image string (without data URI prefix). |
| `k` | number | no |  | Number of unique images to return. If not provided, automatically finds optimal k by looking at diminishing return |

Example:
CallMcpTool({ server: "Jina AI", toolName: "deduplicate_images", arguments: {"images": ["value"]} })

### deduplicate_strings
Get top-k semantically unique strings from a list using Jina embeddings and submodular optimization. Use this when you have many similar strings and want to select the most diverse subset that covers the semantic space. Perfect for removing duplicates, selecting representative samples, or finding diverse content.

| Field | Type | Required | Default | Description |
|-------|------|----------|---------|-------------|
| `strings` | array<string> | yes |  | Array of strings to deduplicate |
| `k` | number | no |  | Number of unique strings to return. If not provided, automatically finds optimal k by looking at diminishing return |

Example:
CallMcpTool({ server: "Jina AI", toolName: "deduplicate_strings", arguments: {"strings": ["value"]} })

### expand_query
Expand and rewrite search queries based on an up-to-date query expansion model. This tool takes an initial query and returns multiple expanded queries that can be used for more diversed and deeper searches. Useful for improving deep research results by searching broader and deeper.

| Field | Type | Required | Default | Description |
|-------|------|----------|---------|-------------|
| `query` | string | yes |  | The search query to expand (e.g., 'machine learning', 'climate change') |

Example:
CallMcpTool({ server: "Jina AI", toolName: "expand_query", arguments: {"query": "search query"} })

### extract_pdf
Extract figures, tables, and equations from PDF documents using layout detection. Perfect for extracting visual elements from academic papers on arXiv or any PDF URL. Returns base64-encoded images of detected elements with metadata.

| Field | Type | Required | Default | Description |
|-------|------|----------|---------|-------------|
| `id` | string | no |  | arXiv paper ID (e.g., '2301.12345' or 'hep-th/9901001'). Either id or url is required. |
| `url` | string | no |  | Direct PDF URL. Either id or url is required. |
| `max_edge` | number | no | `1024` | Maximum edge size for extracted images in pixels (default: 1024) |
| `type` | string | no |  | Filter by float types (comma-separated): figure, table, equation. If not specified, returns all types. |

Example:
CallMcpTool({ server: "Jina AI", toolName: "extract_pdf", arguments: {"id": "value"} })

### guess_datetime_url
Guess the last updated or published datetime of a web page. This tool examines HTTP headers, HTML metadata, Schema.org data, visible dates, JavaScript timestamps, HTML comments, Git information, RSS/Atom feeds, sitemaps, and international date formats to provide the most accurate update time with confidence scores. Returns the best guess timestamp and confidence level.

| Field | Type | Required | Default | Description |
|-------|------|----------|---------|-------------|
| `url` | string | yes |  | The complete HTTP/HTTPS URL of the webpage to guess datetime information |

Example:
CallMcpTool({ server: "Jina AI", toolName: "guess_datetime_url", arguments: {"url": "https://example.com"} })

### parallel_read_url
Read multiple web pages in parallel to extract clean content efficiently. For best results, provide multiple URLs that you need to extract simultaneously. This is useful for comparing content across multiple sources or gathering information from multiple pages at once.

| Field | Type | Required | Default | Description |
|-------|------|----------|---------|-------------|
| `urls` | array<object> | yes |  | Array of URL configurations to read in parallel (maximum 5 URLs for optimal performance) |
| `timeout` | number | no | `30000` | Timeout in milliseconds for all URL reads |

Example:
CallMcpTool({ server: "Jina AI", toolName: "parallel_read_url", arguments: {"urls": [{}]} })

